# Fixed 2026-10-02, defect 135's class, lane literals (the certain-fix
# audit's L4, and the shapes beside it). Symptom: `\%`, written for a
# percent sign, Ruby's `\#{` and Perl's `\@`, which keep an interpolation
# from opening, and JavaScript's `\8` and `\9`, cost `unknown_escape` with
# the certain fix `\\`, so `check --apply` wrote a program that checked
# clean and printed a backslash where the character alone was meant. Cause:
# only `\$`, `\?`, `\/` and `` \` `` were read as a character this language
# writes bare. Now these are too: dropping the backslash and `\\` are both
# guesses, and `--apply` leaves the case as it is (`escape_readings`).

function a_percent_sign()
    print("100\%")  #~ unknown_escape

function an_interpolation_kept_shut()
    print("\#{name} user\@host")  #~ unknown_escape unknown_escape

function a_digit_past_the_octal_ones()
    print("\8\9")  #~ unknown_escape unknown_escape

function in_an_f_literal()
    n = 3
    print(f"{n}\%")  #~ unknown_escape
